<h2>The statutory structure</h2>
<p>Two punishment provisions do most of the work in Arms Act prosecutions. Section 25 punishes, among other things, the acquisition or possession of arms or ammunition without a licence, and several of its clauses carry minimum sentences, which narrows sentencing discretion and raises the stakes of the charge itself. Section 27 punishes the use of arms, with graded severity depending on the manner and consequence of use. The distance between a Section 25 possession case and a Section 27 use case is considerable, and the bail analysis begins by identifying precisely which clause the prosecution invokes.</p>
<p>Two further provisions shape the litigation around these charges. Section 39 requires the previous sanction of the District Magistrate before any prosecution is instituted in respect of an offence under Section 3 — the provision requiring a licence for acquisition and possession. Whether sanction exists, and when it was obtained, is therefore a threshold question in possession cases. Separately, Section 45 carves out exemptions from the operation of the Act for certain categories of articles and persons; where a case arguably falls within an exemption, that is a point going to the root of the prosecution.</p>

<h2>The distinctions that drive bail outcomes</h2>
<p>Arms Act bail hearings are intensely fact-specific, but the facts that matter cluster around a handful of recurring distinctions.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Licence lapse vs unlicensed weapon</h4><p>A licensed weapon held on an expired or non-renewed licence is a different case from recovery of a weapon that was never licensed at all. The first is often characterised as a technical or regulatory default; the second raises the inference of concealment and criminal purpose.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Weapon with live cartridges vs ammunition alone</h4><p>Recovery of a firearm together with live cartridges suggests readiness for use. Recovery of a few cartridges alone, without any weapon, sits at the lower end of gravity, particularly where the quantity is small and there is no suggestion of trafficking.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Standalone recovery vs recovery during another offence</h4><p>A weapon recovered in a standalone search is weighed differently from one recovered during the investigation of a robbery, extortion or shooting. In the latter situation the Arms Act charge travels with the principal offence, and bail is effectively decided on the composite case.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Personal recovery vs disputed possession</h4><p>Recovery from the person of the accused is the strongest form of the prosecution case. Recovery from a shared vehicle, a rented room with multiple occupants, or a public place raises the contested ingredient of conscious possession.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Conscious possession: the contested ingredient</h2>
<p>Possession under the Arms Act is not a purely physical concept. It carries a mental element: the person must be shown to have known of the weapon and to have exercised control over it. This matters most in cases of recovery from premises or vehicles used by several people. Where the prosecution case rests on proximity alone — a pistol under a car seat in a vehicle with four occupants, a weapon in a house occupied by a family — the question of whose possession it was is genuinely triable, and a bail court is entitled to note that the ingredient is contested without conducting a mini-trial.</p>

<h2>The FSL report</h2>
<p>The forensic opinion on the recovered article occupies an outsized place in these cases. The Forensic Science Laboratory is asked whether the article answers the statutory description of a firearm, whether it is in working condition, and whether the ammunition is live. Three situations recur at the bail stage:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Report awaited</h4><p>Where the FSL report is pending months after recovery, prolonged custody rests on an unverified assumption about the weapon. The delay itself becomes a bail consideration.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Weapon non-functional</h4><p>A finding that the article is not in working condition weakens the gravity of the allegation and is squarely relevant to bail, though it does not by itself end the prosecution.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Weapon functional, cartridges live</h4><p>A positive report confirms the prosecution case on the article, and the bail argument shifts to possession, antecedents and the period of custody already undergone.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Sanction under Section 39</h2>
<p>Because Section 39 makes the previous sanction of the District Magistrate a precondition for prosecuting a Section 3 offence, the sanction record is part of the standard checklist in possession cases. The point is usually one for the charge or trial stage rather than a ground that by itself secures bail, but an absent or mechanically granted sanction is legitimately flagged in the bail application as bearing on the strength of the prosecution case.</p>

<h2>Practice in Delhi</h2>
<p>In Delhi, bail applications in Arms Act cases involving unlicensed firearms are ordinarily moved before the Court of Session under Section 483 of the BNSS (formerly Section 439 CrPC), with a status report called from the investigating officer. The application typically addresses: the specific clause invoked and its punishment range; the circumstances of recovery and the presence or absence of independent witnesses to it; the conscious-possession question where recovery was not personal; the stage of the FSL report; antecedents; and the period of custody. Where the Arms Act charge is appended to a principal offence, the bail application deals with the composite allegation rather than treating the weapons charge in isolation.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Conditions imposed on bail in these cases ordinarily include marking attendance with the investigating officer, not contacting recovery witnesses, and not committing a similar offence while on bail. Breach of conditions exposes the accused to cancellation, so conditions should be realistic when sought and scrupulously observed once imposed.</p></div>
